- op: discuss  # faf4
  at: '2026-09-16T07:32:06Z'  # faf4
  by: agent  # faf4
  actor: jgstern_agent  # faf4
  clock: 4  # faf4
  nonce: faf4  # faf4
  message: "DONE 2026-09-16 by PR #997 (8d3bff18c1). Behavioral evidence: this item's own\nrepro, re-run on the merged tree.\n\n    appended a line to hypergumbo_lang_mainstream/bash.py, smart-test --manifest\n\n    before   213 selected, test_module_key_axis.py NOT among them\n    after    230 selected, test_module_key_axis.py PRESENT\n\nTHIS ITEM'S PROPOSED FIX WOULD HAVE MISSED THIS ITEM'S OWN EXAMPLE, which is\nthe finding worth recording. The filing asked for \"a test file that contains a\nstring literal naming a changed source path\". Measured: test_module_key_axis.py\ndoes not contain the string \"bash\" at all. The citation lives in the SOURCE the\ntest reads (module_key_axis.py:306), so one hop is not enough:\n\n  1. find every file whose text contains a literal naming the changed path;\n  2. a citer that IS a test is selected; a citer that is a source or a script\n     contributes the tests that name IT, by its import path.\n\nA one-hop gate would have shipped, measured clean against a tree where nothing\nwas wrong, and left the defect where it was. That is now a named test.\n\nA CITATION HAS A SLASH IN IT, measured rather than assumed. Slash-bearing\nliterals name 12 shipped sources from 9 citing files across the whole tree. The\nbare basename bash.py appears in five test files with nothing to do with the\ncitation, so keying on it would trade a precise edge for noise. Same reasoning\nin hop 2: for hypergumbo_core/taint.py the bare stem matches 142 test files and\nthe import path 64, and both reach the module's own test.\n\nTWO INSTRUMENT FAULTS OF MY OWN, both in the test rather than the fix, both\nfound by CI and worth recording because the second refutes the first.\n  * The test file, written with its subjects spelled out, became a CITER of\n    them -- so two controls silently became assertions about their own text.\n    Paths are assembled from parts now and a guard enforces it; the guard\n    caught a second instance in a docstring on its own first run.\n  * The enumeration reported ZERO citers in CI against 9 locally, twice. The\n    first diagnosis (a glob divergence under Python 3.11) was WRONG. The cause\n    was parts.index(\"src\") on an ABSOLUTE path: CI checks out at\n    /woodpecker/SRC/github.com/iterabloom/hypergumbo, so the search found the\n    workspace's src and every key came out malformed. Reproduced by symlinking\n    the repo under a CI-shaped path. A floor on the population size did not\n    catch it -- 348 sources were found every time -- because a floor guards\n    against an EMPTY instrument, not a MIS-AIMED one. The accessor now asserts\n    the SHAPE of what it produced.\n\nNOT CLOSED BY THIS, and disclosed rather than left to be discovered: docs cite\nshipped sources the same way (7 files under docs/ plus CHANGELOG.md), and none\nis scanned. That is deliberate -- no test today checks a doc's citations\nagainst the tree, so the edge would have no reader. The helper's docstring\nnames where such a gate would attach.\n\nTHE SECOND CLAIMANT NAMED IN THE 2026-09-16 ENTRY IS STILL OPEN.\nKNOWN_UNREACHABLE's test_stdlib_version_guarded_imports.py wants a union\nselecting it on ANY production-source change; this union keys on citations, so\nit does not cover that. It belongs to INV-lizor limb (a)."  # faf4
